            "text": "Rearranges a tensor into a new shape, keeping every number and its order.\n\nTwelve numbers can be one row of twelve, three rows of four, four rows of three or a column of twelve. t.reshape changes which of these you have;\nthe numbers themselves are untouched. Reach for it when a node wants its data in a particular layout - a list of 3-D points as rows of three, an image as rows and columns, a 2-D result as one long row.\n\nHOW THE NUMBERS ARE PLACED:\nThe tensor is read row by row, as it would print, and the new shape is filled row by row in the same order. So a ramp 0 .. 1 reshaped to 3 4 puts the first four values in row 0, the next four in row 1 and so on - the ramp still reads left to right, top to bottom.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.reshape <dim> <dim> ...\nWith no arguments the shape is -1: everything in one row, like t.flatten.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.reshape 3 4\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list, a numpy array or a number is turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu first). Receiving it is what makes the node send.\n\nshape (text box, also an inlet):\nThe new shape. Any whole numbers in the text are the dimensions, so 3 4, [3, 4] and 3,4 all mean three rows of four. From a cord it takes a string (as from the demo's string boxes), a list of numbers or a tensor of whole numbers. The dimensions must multiply to the number of values you have: 3 4 works for twelve and fails for ten. One dimension may be -1, meaning \"whatever fits\": 6 -1 gives six rows of however many columns are needed. An empty box passes the tensor through unchanged. Changing the shape does NOT send by itself - the next tensor uses it. In the demo the metro re-sends the ramp, so a click shows at once. If the shape does not fit, a 'Shape Mismatch' message is printed and nothing is sent; the heat_map keeps its last picture.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe same numbers in the new shape, same dtype and device.\n\nRESHAPE OR VIEW?\nt.reshape always works. When the tensor's memory is already in reading order it gives a view of the same memory; otherwise (after t.t or t.transpose, say) it quietly copies. t.view does only the first and refuses the second - see its page.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe strip at the top is the ramp as it arrives: shape [12], one row. Click 3 4 and the heat_map below shows it folded into three rows of four, the values still in order reading across each row. 4 3 and 2 6 fold it differently; 6 -1 lets t.reshape work out the 2; 12 1 makes a column - twelve rows of one value, the ramp now running down the picture. t.info prints each result's shape.\n\nRELATED:\nt.view is the same refold, but only without copying. t.flatten goes back to one row. t.squeeze and t.unsqueeze remove or add a single axis of length 1 without spelling out the whole shape. t.transpose is NOT a reshape: it turns rows into columns, which moves the numbers. np.reshape is the numpy version."
        },
